 ## Subtopics
 
-### Jaccard Index Evaluation
-A metric used to evaluate voxel segmentation quality by computing the Jaccard index between target and predicted voxel grids [S2, unofficial]. It is mathematically identical to IoU. It is chosen when working directly with voxel grids rather than projected masks. Implementation involves computing the intersection and union of `target.voxel` and `pred.voxel` based on `cfg.num_classes` [S2, unofficial].
+### [Jaccard Index Evaluation](17997-jaccard-index-evaluation.md)
+Evaluates voxel segmentation quality by computing the Jaccard index between target and predicted voxel grids. It measures spatial overlap between binary or multi-class voxel sets and requires exact voxel-wise agreement, serving as a stricter alternative to correlation-based metrics.
+
+### Silhouette IoU Metric
+Computes IoU between the predicted mask (generated by thresholding pixel opacity at 0.5) and the un-eroded ground-truth mask [S3, unofficial]. It quantifies misalignment between renders and ground truth and is typically reported only when test-time camera refinement is applied [S3, unofficial].
+
+### Alpha IoU
+A soft IoU variant measuring agreement between predicted foreground alpha and ground-truth mask [S4, unofficial]. It computes the ratio of summed minimums to summed maximums over all pixels: $IoU(\hat{A}, M) = \frac{\sum_v \min(\hat{A}(v), M(v))}{\sum_v \max(\hat{A}(v), M(v))}$, where $\hat{A}(v)$ is predicted alpha and $M(v)$ is the ground-truth mask at pixel $v$ [S4, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of voxels or pixels in the mask, as it requires a single pass to count $tp, fp, fn$.
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ auxiliary space if counts are accumulated, or $O(N)$ if masks are stored in memory.
-- **Strengths:** Simple to compute; intuitive interpretation of spatial overlap; robust to scale if masks are normalized [S1, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** Insensitive to the magnitude of error (e.g., a slight boundary shift vs. a large hole may have similar IoU if the overlap ratio is similar); ignores true negatives, which can be misleading in highly imbalanced scenes [S1, unofficial].
-- **When to use:** When evaluating binary segmentation quality of rendered depth maps or voxel grids [S1, S2, unofficial].
-- **When not to use:** When detailed error analysis (e.g., distinguishing between floaters and missing geometry) is required; use TPR/FPR alongside IoU [S1, unofficial].
+- **Time Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
+- **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
+- **Strengths:** Measures overall alignment between predicted and ground-truth masks [S2, unofficial].
+- **Limitations:** Does not distinguish between different sources of error, such as floaters versus missing geometry [S2, unofficial].
+- **When to use:** Evaluating binary segmentation quality of rendered depth maps or voxel grids [S2, unofficial].
+- **When not to use:** When detailed error source analysis is required; use alongside True Positive Rate (TPR), False Positive Rate (FPR), and False Discovery Rate (FDR) [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-IoU originated in computer vision for object detection and segmentation evaluation. In the context of volume rendering and neural view synthesis, it has been adopted to assess the geometric accuracy of implicit and primitive-based representations by comparing thresholded rendered depth maps against ground-truth masks [S1, unofficial]. It is a standard metric in space robotics and NVS (Neural View Synthesis) benchmarks [S1, unofficial].
+In volume rendering and neural view synthesis, IoU assesses the geometric accuracy of implicit and primitive-based representations by comparing thresholded rendered depth maps against ground-truth masks [S2, unofficial]. It is applied in space-focused evaluation pipelines to quantify reconstruction alignment [S2, unofficial].
 
